course: dictionary
lesson: 4922
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Immunity is one of them. In science it means the body's ability to fight off a disease. In law it can mean protection from being punished, as in «diplomatic immunity».
explain: «When nearly everyone in a class is immune, a germ like measles can't find enough people to jump to.» The clues are germs, measles and the jab, so immunity here is about the body fighting disease. Ask yourself which meaning fits, then try it in the sentence.
explain: Context also shows you the feeling or idea behind a word. «A pandemic is a new disease spreading across many countries because nobody has immunity yet.» Nobody has immunity, so the disease can spread, and that tells you what immunity protects us from.
fact: Immunity is a noun, and its plural is immunities.
fact: «Immunity» can mean the body's ability to fight off a disease so that you do not catch it.
fact: «Herd immunity» means that when most people are immune, a germ can't spread easily.
fact: For measles, about 95 in every 100 people need to be immune for herd immunity.
fact: «Pandemic» means a new disease spreading across many countries because nobody has immunity yet.
exercise: The doctor said the jab would help you build up immunity. What does immunity mean here? | A pet that never bites | A bag for carrying books | A free ticket to the match | *The body's ability to fight off a disease
exercise: «Some people have diplomatic immunity from being charged.» In law, what does immunity mean? | Being very rich | Being the fastest runner | A new sort of disease | *Protection from being punished or from a law
exercise: «A pandemic is a new disease spreading across many countries because nobody has immunity yet.» What is a pandemic? | A big party in a class | A germ that cannot spread | A type of vaccine for babies | *A new disease spreading across many countries
test: For measles, about how many in every 100 people need to be immune for herd immunity? | *95 | 20 | 50 | 10
test: «Herd immunity protects babies too young for the jab.» Why? | They are already older | They are vaccinated by the germ | They get extra sleep | *A germ can't spread easily to them
exam: Which use of immunity means the body fights off a disease? | Immunity from being charged by the police | *Immunity to a disease, so you cannot catch it | Immunity from the school bell | Immunity from being out in a game
